        <qudt:applicableUnit rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/CCY_YER"/>
        <qudt:applicableUnit rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/CCY_ZAR"/>
        <qudt:applicableUnit rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/CCY_ZMW"/>
        <qudt:applicableUnit rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/CCY_ZWL"/>
        <qudt:applicableUnit rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/unit/MegaCCY_USD"/>
        <qudt:hasDimensionVector rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/dimensionvector/A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1"/>
        <qudt:specializationOf rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/Asset"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind/Asset"/>
        <qudt:dbpediaMatch rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">http://dbpedia.org/resource/Currency</qudt:dbpediaMatch>
        <qudt:informativeReference rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#anyURI">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Currency</qudt:informativeReference>
        <qudt:plainTextDescription>In economics, currency is a generally accepted medium of exchange. These are usually the coins and
    banknotes of a particular government, which comprise the physical aspects of a nation&apos;s money supply. The other part of a
    nation&apos;s money supply consists of bank deposits (sometimes called deposit money), ownership of which can be transferred by
    means of cheques, debit cards, or other forms of money transfer. Deposit money and currency are money in the sense that both
    are acceptable as a means of payment.
  </qudt:plainTextDescription>
        <rdfs:comment>Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Currency</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Currency</rdfs:label>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


